(vector
 (map
  (:adr/id "adr-2609112200-a-canned-reading-substituted-without-checking-the-measurement")
  (:adr/title "A canned reading substituted without checking the measurement")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-11")
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609112100-the-rule-against-inventing-fixtures-does-not-cover-a-whole-oracle"))
  (:adr/body "
## What this iteration did

Rolled the oracle-store count layer out from one pilot to all sixteen
generated harnesses, and fixed two defects found on the way. Both defects
have the shape this workspace keeps recording: something was written, it
looked right, and it said something untrue.

## Reproduce the rollout

For each generated pilot, from an origin/main worktree in the scratchpad
(never the shared orgs/ checkout):

    nbb <kotoba-lang>/scripts/gen-parity-harness.cljs <worktree> <src-dir>
    cd <worktree>
    kbb --backend sci --classpath src:<kotoba-lang/text>/src q9-oracle.cljs
    amu check   src/<src>/whole_component.kotoba --policy policy.edn
    amu compile src/<src>/whole_component.kotoba --target aarch64-macos
                --policy policy.edn --output wc.o
    amu extract-native wc.o --symbol oracle-store --output store.bin
    for s in 0 3 4 5 7; do
      KEXE_FUEL=100000000 ./kexe-loader store.bin <offset> 1 aarch64 <caps> $s
    done

<offset> is the :offset the extract step prints. <caps> are the capability
ids in that repository's own policy.edn, not a constant. Both sides must
produce the same five values, and those five must not be all one value.

Two flags are easy to get wrong and fail in unrelated-looking ways:
extract-native takes the COMPILED artifact, not the source (given the
source it reports EDN input contains trailing forms), and both check and
compile need --policy or admission denies the two declared capabilities.

## The control is a mutation, not a cross-comparison

Every pilot yields the identical 3,2,3,3,0. That is correct, because the
selectors test persistence semantics and those do not vary by repository.
But it means agreement across repositories proves nothing, and comparing
two different selectors only shows that two different questions have
different answers.

So the layer was seen red by breaking the component. In a scratch copy of
com-airtable, the absent-id guard in store-retract was changed to remove
row 0 instead of returning the store unchanged, then recompiled and rerun:

    selector 4 alone moves 3 -> 2; selectors 0, 3, 5 and 7 hold.

That is the semantic the layer claims to test, retract an id that is not
there and the count must not move, and it is the only one that moved.

## Defect 1: the harness said the store layer was not generated

The generator wrote a docstring listing the store, filter, pagination,
handler and expand layers as NOT generated, forty lines above the store
layer it emits. Every generated harness carried the contradiction,
including the one landed in the previous iteration together with the store
layer itself. The docstring now says what that iteration actually decided:
the unit is a SELECTOR, not a layer.

## Defect 2: a field whose value was the name of its own variable

Twelve of twenty landed migration-record.edn files carried a literal
dollar-R-E-A-D-I-N-G string where the reading of the expand oracle
belongs. The cause is one character in the shell that wrote them: an
unquoted heredoc in which that variable was written with a leading
backslash, so the backslash suppressed the one expansion while the
neighbouring values expanded correctly. Both branches of the script
computed a careful, honest reading; neither ever reached a file.

This is the harder half of the class already documented here. A MISSING
field is visible. A field holding the variable's own name is a string: it
passes the reader, passes a string? type assert, passes the parse gate,
and reads at a glance as though a reading is present. The type check
adopted for the (str ...) in EDN defect does not catch it, because nothing
about the type is wrong.

Reproduce, per repository:

    git show origin/main:migration-record.edn | grep -c READING

## Why the repair had to be measured, not pasted

The obvious repair is to substitute back the branch text the script would
have produced, chosen by ref-count. Doing that would have shipped a false
sentence.

The ref-bearing reading asserts that selector 4 equals selector 0 because
the ref entity carries exactly one ref. One pilot, com-airbus-analytx, has
ref-count 2, and its own recorded values show selector 4 differing from
selector 0, with six selectors yielding four distinct values. The canned
text even names com-abb-robotics as the repository where this differs, and
would have been pasted unchanged into a second one.

So the repair re-derives every clause from the values in the record before
choosing a text, and REFUSES with exit 2 rather than writing when the
floor it is about to assert does not hold. Three readings resulted, and
which one a repository gets is decided by its own measurement.

Reproduce the discrimination:

    for r in com-airtable com-agones com-airbus-analytx; do
      gh api repos/kotoba-lang/$r/contents/migration-record.edn --jq .content
        | base64 -d | grep -oE 'UNEXERCISED|carries exactly one|carries TWO refs'
    done

Three repositories, three different answers. A repair that produced the
same text for all three would be indistinguishable from one that never
read the values.

## Three bad probes in one iteration, all mine

Recorded because the same reflex caught all three and no gate did:

1. A scan for the nine vars the harness calls reported all nine missing in
   all twenty repositories. grep is line-based, so a trailing whitespace
   class never matches a var whose arglist starts on the next line. That
   is the com-8th-wall defect already fixed IN THE GENERATOR, reintroduced
   in the probe that was checking the generator.
2. A layer-presence scan reported no route layer in files that have one.
   The row is labelled main(route-count), not route.
3. A table checking the reading's claims used positional splitting of an
   unquoted variable in zsh, which does not word-split, so all three claim
   columns compared empty strings and printed a uniform, vacuous verdict.

In each case the tell was uniformity. A column that reads the same for
every row has usually not been measured.

## A note on checking this document

Balanced parentheses and an even number of quotes is not a reader. This
ADR passed both and the ADL reader still refused it with unsupported
escape in string, because the first draft quoted the placeholder with
backslash-escaped quotes and used backslash line-continuations in the
shell examples. Read the file back with kotoba.adl/read-string and inspect
the parsed value, not the byte counts.

## What is landed

Sixteen generated harnesses carry the five count-valued store selectors,
each verified against its own component compiled to aarch64-macos and
executed. Twelve migration records carry a reading derived from their own
values. Seventeen west pins advanced in one commit.

## What is still refused

The three hash-valued selectors of oracle-store, and the filter,
pagination, handler and expand layers. None has been read selector by
selector.

The four hand-written harnesses (com-aadhaar, com-aave, com-abb-robotics,
com-accela) are richer than the generator's output and are not
regenerated. That list is FOUR, not the two recorded in earlier
iterations: com-aadhaar and com-aave were found to carry every layer when
the cohort was measured rather than recalled.
")))
